  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Tranquil weather continues through Saturday, though\na few very isolated instability/sea breeze showers will occur this\nafternoon.\n\nSurface high pressure settles in later today with drier air moving\nin. Radar is bearing out earlier guidance which hinted at a few\nrather isolated showers this afternoon, mainly focused along \nthe sea and bay breezes. So, kept some mainly slight chance \nPOPs along the coastal strip into this evening. A large majority\nwill stay dry however given the closed low/trough continuing to\nmove away. Any showers dissipate early this evening with a \nquiet night unfolding. Temperatures warm some more to start the \nweekend with high temperatures on Saturday right near average \nalong with dry conditions. Guidance again hints that a few \nisolated pop-up showers could develop but signal is weak and so \nis forcing, so kept forecast mostly dry for now except perhaps \nan isolated convergence shower in the Delmarva.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Showers and thunderstorms increase Sunday night\nand Monday with the potential for areas of heavy rain with some\nflash flooding risk.\n\nAn upper-level trough will shift across eastern Canada this weekend \nand early next week. While this occurs, strong energy crests a ridge \nin the West and this carves the trough out more into the Tennessee \nand Ohio valleys by later this weekend. This may result in the \ntrough becoming closed off into early next week as it gradually \napproaches the East. The main trough may detach and depart to the \nnortheast leaving behind a lingering closed low. This scenario would \nlead to the system slowing its eastward progression through the \nfirst half of next week. The model trends continue to slow the \narrival of this system, with the bulk of the showers and \nthunderstorms occurring later Sunday night and especially on Monday.\n\nAhead of the strengthening upper-level trough, deep south to \nsouthwest flow will increase and this will ramp up the low-level \nmoisture and temperature advection. It will therefore be a humid \ntime frame as the precipitable waters surge to 2+ inches. There may \nbe multiple rounds of showers and thunderstorms, starting with an \ninitial one later Sunday night into Monday with the increased warm \nair advection regime. Instability will increase, however the timing \nat night should limit the thunderstorm intensity especially since it \nmay be more of elevated convection initially. Areas of heavy rain \nhowever will be probable given the increasing forcing for ascent and \nhigh water content air. Some ripples of energy along an incoming \ncold front during Monday may enhance the convective potential and \nwith deep southerly flow, some training convection will be possible. \nExcessive rainfall rates would enhance the flash flooding risk, \nespecially from the I-95 corridor to the north and west where the\nelevation rise could boost the ascent some given a southeast to south\nlow-level flow. There is a Slight Risk (level 2 out of 4) for excessive\nrainfall Monday and Monday night for most of our eastern Pennsylvania\ncounties, most of New Jersey and into northern Delaware. There is\na Marginal Risk (level 1 out of 4) for the rest of our area. If enough\nheating develops during the course of Monday, that combined with\nsome increase in shear (some initially backed low- level flow) could\nlead to at least some severe thunderstorm risk. This potential is\nless certain though given the chance for multiple rounds of \nconvection/cloud cover, and a moist atmospheric profile which tends\nto limit the overall instability.\n\nGiven the slow eastward progression of this system, the chances for \nconvection look to continue for multiple days next week as the cold \nfront may stall in our vicinity. The current forecast does have \nshower and thunderstorm chances each day next week, mainly in the \nafternoon and evening. The most active day, as of now, however looks \nto be Monday given the overlap of favorable parameters for more \nwidespread convective development.",
